        "original_id": "d49b187e-136f-5c9b-9c86-df579d6deb54",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Lee Unkrich, Robert Stevenson, Walt Disney, Sherman Brothers, Don DaGradi, Bill Walsh\n- Companies: Pixar Animation Studios, Walt Disney Pictures\n- Products: Monsters, Inc., Mary Poppins, Finding Nemo, Toy Story 3\n- Awards: Academy Award for Best Animated Film, Oscar nomination for Best Adapted Screenplay\n\nFacts:\n- Mary Poppins is a 1964 American musical-fantasy film directed by Robert Stevenson and produced by Walt Disney, with songs written and composed by the Sherman Brothers.\n- Don DaGradi’s visual screenplay for Mary Poppins earned him and Bill Walsh an Oscar nomination for Best Adapted Screenplay in 1964.\n- Monsters, Inc. is a 2001 American computer-animated comedy film produced by Pixar Animation Studios and distributed by Walt Disney Pictures.\n- Lee Unkrich co-directed Monsters, Inc. and Finding Nemo before making his solo directorial debut with Toy Story 3.\n- Lee Unkrich won the Academy Award for Best Animated Film in 2011 for Toy Story 3.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "1a52dc94-c4ad-5886-af24-cdd1a529171d",
        "text": "Page: Monsters, Inc.. Sentence: Monsters, Inc. is a 2001 American computer-animated comedy film produced by Pixar Animation Studios and distributed by Walt Disney Pictures.\nPage: Mary Poppins (film). Sentence: Mary Poppins is a 1964 American musical-fantasy film directed by Robert Stevenson and produced by Walt Disney, with songs written and composed by the Sherman Brothers.\nPage: Lee Unkrich. Sentence: After co-directing \"Monsters, Inc.\" and \"Finding Nemo\", Unkrich made his solo directorial debut with \"Toy Story 3\", for which he won the Academy Award for Best Animated Film in 2011.\nPage: Don DaGradi. Sentence: His greatest achievement was for his visual screenplay for \"Mary Poppins\" in 1964 for which he shared an Oscar nomination for Best Adapted Screenplay with Bill Walsh.",
